XGBoost Performance Analysis
We evaluated the XGBoost model on five crime categories: THEFT, BATTERY, CRIMINAL_DAMAGE, ASSAULT, and DECEPTIVE_PRACTICE. The model was trained using the past 30 days of spatiotemporal crime patterns to predict crime counts for the corresponding time slots of the next day. Performance was assessed using MAE and RMSE on both the validation and test sets.
Overall, the model achieved stable and consistent performance across all five crime types. The average validation RMSE was 0.1028, while the average test RMSE was 0.0993. Similarly, the average validation MAE and test MAE were 0.0167 and 0.0155, respectively. The close agreement between validation and test errors suggests that the model generalizes well to unseen data and does not exhibit obvious overfitting.
Among the five categories, THEFT was the most difficult to predict, with a test RMSE of 0.1346, while DECEPTIVE_PRACTICE achieved the lowest test RMSE of 0.0671. This pattern is reasonable, as theft-related incidents are often more dynamic and influenced by more complex environmental and human mobility factors, whereas some other categories may show more stable temporal patterns. Despite these differences, all five models converged successfully and reached reasonable stopping points, indicating that the feature design and training setup were effective.
Another important observation is that the test errors are consistently close to, and in some cases slightly lower than, the validation errors. This indicates that the selected lag, rolling, temporal, and neighborhood features are able to capture meaningful spatiotemporal dependencies in the crime data. In particular, the results suggest that recent local history and nearby spatial context provide useful signals for next-day crime forecasting.
In summary, the XGBoost model provides a strong and reliable baseline for this task. It is able to learn useful nonlinear relationships from structured spatiotemporal features and produces robust performance across different crime categories. These results show that tree-based models can be highly effective for short-term crime count forecasting, especially when carefully designed lag and neighborhood features are incorporated.
